Clinically Isolated Syndrome (CIS)
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
First monophasic demyelinating event | Objective neuro deficit >24 hours | MRI white matter lesions (Dawson's fingers)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-003
Reason for Visit: Acute onset visual loss and focal weakness requiring urgent neurological evaluation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate a rapid deterioration in vision and motor function. Immediate clinical intervention was necessary to exclude acute ischemic stroke or rapidly progressive myelopathy prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My left eye suddenly hurts when I move it and everything is blurry, plus my leg feels too weak to hold my weight.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a first clinical episode characteristic of inflammatory demyelination. Symptoms began 36 hours ago and have progressively worsened. The patient reports unilateral optic neuritis with painful vision loss in the left eye. This is accompanied by new focal limb weakness and sensory disturbance in the right lower extremity. The patient describes Lhermitte's phenomenon, explicitly noting electrical sensations down the spine upon neck flexion. The spouse notes recent ataxia and balance impairment. The patient admits to mild bowel and bladder dysfunction, specifically urinary hesitancy. There is a distinct exacerbation of symptoms with heat, notably after a hot shower earlier today.
Functional Status: Currently requires assistance to mobilize safely due to unilateral weakness and visual deficit.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate fall risk secondary to combined visual and motor deficits.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 mmHg | HR: 74 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 14 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: The patient appears exhausted, guarding the left eye, and visibly distressed by the neurological changes.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals reduced visual acuity in the left eye with pain on extraocular movement. Internuclear ophthalmoplegia is observed on lateral gaze. Motor exam shows 4/5 strength in the right leg. Gait is wide-based and ataxic. Systemic assessment reveals profound persistent fatigue, obvious post-exertional malaise following the physical exam, and significant brain fog impacting the patient's ability to recall exact symptom timelines.
Environmental Observations: Multi-level home with stairs presenting a severe navigational hazard given current ataxia.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No relevant chronic medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Clinically Isolated Syndrome (CIS) - Status: Exacerbated, acute initial presentation.
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out due to the gradual 36-hour evolution, exacerbation with heat, and Lhermitte's phenomenon, which strongly indicate demyelination rather than vascular ischemia.

Compressive Myelopathy - Ruled out due to the concurrent presence of optic neuritis, indicating central, disseminated neurological involvement above the spinal cord.

Systemic Infection/Meningitis - Ruled out due to the absence of fever, normocardia, and isolated neurological symptom clusters without systemic toxicity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This complex presentation demanded an urgent after-hours home assessment due to the sudden onset of focal neurological deficits and painful vision loss. Telephone triage correctly prioritized the risk of an acute cerebrovascular event or rapidly progressive spinal cord pathology. Detailed physical examination isolated the pathology to distinct central nervous system tracts characteristic of demyelination, ruling out an acute, life-threatening ischemic stroke. By conducting this thorough assessment, the immediate risk of catastrophic neurological collapse was excluded, allowing for a structured, safe referral pathway for neuroimaging rather than an uncoordinated emergency department surge overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Facilitated an urgent outpatient neurology referral. The expected investigation results include single or multiple T2-hyperintense white matter lesions on MRI without fulfilling criteria for dissemination in time, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent heat-induced symptom flares, schedule high-energy tasks for the cooler morning hours; successfully completing tasks without fatigue will reinforce pacing strategies and maximize your functional independence.
Home-Based Services: Physiotherapy referral for mobility assessment and gait training.
DME/Safety: Issue a single-point walking stick to increase base of support and immediate instruction to avoid using stairs unassisted.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24 hours to expedite the MRI and neurology pathways.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if there is a sudden progression to complete paralysis, profound difficulty breathing, inability to swallow, or total loss of vision.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal neuroimaging and specialist review; neurological conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course for this episode is a gradual stabilization followed by partial or complete recovery over weeks, though future episodes remain possible.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if your weakness spreads to your arms, you develop sudden slurred speech, or if you become unable to swallow liquids.

